00:04:22.945 --> 00:04:24.190
JEANA JORGENSEN: Fantastic.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:24.970
OK.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.580
Before we get on to the details
of how Community Day will run,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.680
I have got two
questions for you.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.040
OK, first question,
Google Cloud has

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.582
been talking about empathetic
engineering for quite some time

00:04:35.582 --> 00:04:36.082
now.

00:04:36.082 --> 00:04:38.380
Can you tell us a little bit
more about what that means

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.672
and why you think it strengthens
developer experiences?

00:04:40.672 --> 00:04:42.070
URS HOLZLE: Sure.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.830
By empathetic
engineering, we really

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:47.530
mean, how are we
putting ourselves

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:50.380
in the shoes of our
users, the developers,

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.050
and ensure that we build
products and services that

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.780
really start with
your needs first?

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:57.980
We've always been an
engineering-led company.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.980
So what we needed
to learn was really

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:04.810
how you wanted to use our
services because users come

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:07.370
with different challenges
and different backgrounds

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.220
from different
industries, some of them

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.910
new to Cloud technologies.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.750
So that's where customer
empathy comes in.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:17.740
As an engineer, do you really
understand the experience

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:19.960
that real users have
with your products?

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.290
Are you really walking
in their shoes?

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:26.260
So we implemented a
listening and learning system

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.660
based on customer empathy.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.750
So for example,
teams test products

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:36.700
by performing common user
journeys just the same way

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.810
customers would without internal
help, without internal tools,

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.370
without insider tips.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:43.600
We gather the feedback.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.570
We improve the services
or the documentation

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:48.490
and then test it
with real customers.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:51.430
So we keep iterating until
we're confident that we've

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.860
built intuitive
services that are

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.320
easy for developers
to implement and build

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:57.672
great solutions around.

00:05:57.672 --> 00:05:58.630
JEANA JORGENSEN: Great.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.840
Thank you for that.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.400
So here's my second
question for you.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.850
If you were starting your
career in technology today,

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.740
where would you
focus your learning?

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.200
URS HOLZLE: Wow,
that's a huge question.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.630
Because technology
evolves so quickly

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.400
that it can be
pretty overwhelming,

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.280
especially if you're
early in your career,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.420
and unfortunately because of
the digital transformation

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.740
of many industries, the
success of those organizations

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:28.030
will increasingly depend on
the services and products that

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.370
are built by its developers,
so learning really

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.050
is super important.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.540
Based on what I've
seen, I would probably

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.680
start with these three things.

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:41.090
First, you have to dedicate time
for learning every single week,

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:41.590
right?

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:46.120
So I'd start out by determining
what forms of learning work

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.810
for you independently
of the subject and then

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:55.180
really allocate a set of hours
to grow your knowledge base.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.130
So for some, reading works best.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.120
For some, it's videos.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.537
For some, it's
interactive events.

00:06:59.537 --> 00:07:03.080
You just got to understand
what works out for you.

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:06.460
And so next, I would pick
a couple of topic areas

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.440
that really interest
you and start

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.270
learning the fundamentals.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.000
Joining online
communities or forums

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:16.990
can be a really good way to
get different perspectives

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:20.860
on the subject that maybe aren't
in the standard traditional

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.220
learning paths.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.160
And then finally, of
course, the question

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.400
is, well, what subjects
should you go after?

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.240
Now, there's hundreds of them.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.540
And so that's really
something that depends on you

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:33.590
and your preferences
and your situation.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:36.580
But I'd say three
areas kind of come

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:39.070
to mind for me, first
customer empathy.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:40.520
I already talked about it.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.720
It's not something
that's taught in schools,

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.500
but it's really,
really important

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:47.720
that we build technology that
people actually want to use,

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:48.220
right?

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.800
And so when design
a product, and you

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.920
start with a deep understanding
what the customer actually

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:57.190
needs, then you can really
build more than just a solution.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:00.310
You can create a viable
business and a viable product

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:01.480
that really lasts.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:06.130
Second, learning what it
takes to ship a product,

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.410
also not broadly
taught in schools,

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:12.100
it's really invaluable in
almost every job that I've seen.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:15.520
So that includes things like
the fundamentals of release

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.550
processes, lean or
agile development,

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:22.750
and how you best design products
for self-service delivery

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.660
because you want to
design your product such

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.120
that users can start
using it quickly

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:32.860
rather than depend on some
complicated instructions

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.370
or deployment process.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.539
And then last but
not least, security.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.190
You've all heard about
the growing cyber threats

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:43.140
that so many
organizations are facing.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.500
And they're only going to get
more sophisticated and more

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.340
invasive.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.710
And so security is
something that you

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:53.268
need to build into your product
and in your development process

00:08:53.268 --> 00:08:54.060
from the beginning.

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.750
You can't bolt it on later.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:59.190
So learn about the fundamentals
of securing a product

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:01.680
or system, and then
keep up with the trends.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.920
So that will give
you the confidence

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:09.492
that you need to build long-term
viable products or companies.

00:09:09.492 --> 00:09:10.450
JEANA JORGENSEN: Right.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.230
Really great advice.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:12.022
Thank you for that.

00:09:12.022 --> 00:09:14.430
I know it'll be especially
important for new developers

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:15.810
and early-in-career folks.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.028
So thank you.

00:09:17.028 --> 00:09:18.570
I mentioned earlier
that I'd give you

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.060
a quick rundown of Community
today and how it would unfold.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:22.290
So let's do that now.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:24.870
Right after this, there is
a special spotlight session

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:26.490
featuring Jim
Hogan, a health care

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.160
strategist in Google
Cloud alongside Vint

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:33.210
Cerf, the co-creator of TCP/IP
and who has often been coined

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.095
the father of the internet.

00:09:35.095 --> 00:09:36.720
The two will have a
candid conversation

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.262
about accessibility
in the workplace.

00:09:38.262 --> 00:09:40.012
We think it's going
to be a great session.

00:09:40.012 --> 00:09:42.360
And we really, really are
excited to have you join us.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.600
After that, we'll head into
the heart of Community Day.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.150
You can join us in an immersive
gathering experience called

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:51.630
Kumospace, which creates virtual
campus with meeting rooms,

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.900
smaller spaces to hang out
for one-on-one conversations,

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:58.140
and then larger auditoriums
for more common topics.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:09:59.670
Every single part
of the day will

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.790
be powered by Google
Cloud, including Kumospace.

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:04.440
So the day kicks off
with conversations

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.930
led by Google Developer
Groups across Europe, Middle

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.390
East, and India, and
then the rest of the day,

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:10.890
we'll have a variety
of experiences

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:12.960
and community gatherings
with a few topics

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.520
available at each time block.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:16.110
The first time
block, you can attend

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.420
discussions led by Googlers
and community members

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:21.480
like our Google Developer
Experts and expert innovators

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.900
dedicated to specific
technologies like Vertex AI,

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:28.080
secure software supply
chain, Flutter, or Workspace.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.470
And then the second block, you
can follow a specific community

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:34.680
pathway and spend time with
specialized groups like Kaggle

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.260
data scientists or
Women Techmakers or even

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.700
region-specific groups.

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:41.220
And the third and final
track throughout the day

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.500
will have dedicated
spaces for learning--

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.630
how you can earn a
Cloud certification,

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:47.790
develop new skills in
hands-on lab sessions,

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.730
or even apply your skills for
good and philanthropic-related

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.160
competition with
other attendees.

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:54.660
We really look
forward to seeing you

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:57.070
and hope you enjoy
the rest of the event.

00:10:57.070 --> 00:10:59.550
Join us next for the
DEI spotlight session

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:00.690
with Jim and Vint.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.270
Thank you so much for spending
the last few days with us.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.580
Remember to sign up for
Google Cloud innovators

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:07.568
and become part of our
Google Developer family.

00:11:07.568 --> 00:11:10.110
We're excited to get to know
you and spend more time with you

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:10.960
in the future.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.160
Enjoy the rest of Next.

